# Bitcoin Lifeboat - Heir Recovery Plan (Liana Timelock)

_A plain-language plan for a Liana wallet with a timelocked recovery path._

```
You are reading a recovery plan written by someone who trusts you
to help. This document does not contain bitcoin. It does not contain
secret recovery words. It only contains instructions.

You will not be able to recover anything using only this document.
You will also need:
  - The wallet's hardware device(s) or backup card(s)
  - Any secret words the owner wrote down separately (if they did)
  - Patience: never let anyone rush you

If anyone tells you they can help you "decrypt" or "unlock" this
plan for a fee, hang up. They cannot. They are a scammer.

If you get stuck, stop. Take your time. Find a trusted person who
understands Bitcoin and ask for help. The owner has likely listed
a trusted helper on this page.
```

## Words to know

- Recovery words: the list of words (usually 12 or 24) the owner wrote down by hand. Sometimes called a seed phrase. They are the real key to the money. Never type them into a website.
- Hardware device: a small gadget that holds the recovery words offline and approves payments. Sometimes called a hardware wallet or a signing device.
- Recovery details: a long line of text (often starting with letters like "wpkh" or "wsh", and known to experts as a descriptor) that tells wallet software how to find this wallet. It is not a secret key, but keep it private.
- Wallet software: a free app (for example Sparrow or Bitcoin Core) that shows the wallet and helps approve a payment.
- Timelock: a waiting period measured in Bitcoin blocks or time. The recovery path should be tried only after the wallet software says the waiting period has passed.
- Primary path and recovery path: the primary path is the owner's normal way to spend. The recovery path is the backup route for the heir after the timelock.

## 1. Safety warning

Never type recovery words into a website, chat, email, or a tool that is not the wallet software named by the owner.

This plan does not contain recovery words or private keys. It explains which materials to gather and when the recovery path may be used.

Anyone who asks to "unlock" this plan for a fee is trying to steal from the wallet.

## 2. Do not panic

A timelocked recovery cannot be rushed. Read the whole plan before using any signing device.

If the recovery path is not active yet, wait. Trying early can create confusing errors and wasted work.

Take a break before each signing step. The goal is a correct rehearsal, not a fast one.

## 3. Stop and call a trusted helper

If any path, block height, or wallet message is unclear, stop before signing.

Ask a trusted helper who understands Liana or Bitcoin recovery to review the plan with you.

Trusted helper to call: ______________________

Second reviewer, if required: ______________________

## 4. What you will need

- [ ] This plan, printed on paper.

- [ ] The Liana backup file or written recovery details.

- [ ] The primary-path signing device, if it is still available.

- [ ] The recovery-path device or backup named by the owner.

- [ ] The wallet software listed in section 10.

- [ ] A current block height from a trusted source, written by hand.

## 5. What kind of wallet this is

This is a Liana-style timelock wallet. It has a normal owner path and at least one recovery path that waits for a timelock.

- Script type: wsh(or_d timelock)

- Number of keys listed: 2

Use the primary path first if the owner is available and the primary device still works. Use the recovery path only when the owner planned for it and the waiting period has passed.

## 6. The wallet's recovery details

The recovery details describe both the primary path and the recovery path. Without them, Liana may not be able to show when recovery is available.

Keep a written or stamped copy with the Liana backup package. The details are not recovery words, but they reveal wallet structure, so keep them private.

Recovery details: [descriptor redacted, see private export]

